WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.091
Harvest-now, decrypt-later planning requires a basic understanding of cryptographic roles.

00:00:04.091 --> 00:00:10.077
Public-key cryptography helps systems establish trust or shared secrets without already having a shared secret.

00:00:10.077 --> 00:00:14.867
It appears in certificates, key exchange, digital signatures, TLS handshakes, VPNs, SSH,

00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:17.660
secure email, code signing, and identity systems.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:22.449
Some widely used public-key algorithms are the main focus of quantum-related migration

00:00:22.449 --> 00:00:27.239
planning because their security assumptions may not hold against sufficiently capable quantum

00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:27.889
computers.

00:00:27.889 --> 00:00:30.283
Symmetric encryption has a different role.

00:00:30.283 --> 00:00:35.871
Once two parties share a secret key, symmetric algorithms can protect bulk data efficiently.

00:00:35.871 --> 00:00:40.660
In TLS, for example, public-key mechanisms help set up the protected session,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.252
while symmetric encryption protects application data after the handshake.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:47.444
Hashing and message authentication also have important roles.

00:00:47.444 --> 00:00:50.637
These functions are affected differently by quantum considerations,

00:00:50.637 --> 00:00:55.027
so teams should avoid treating all cryptography as one undifferentiated category.

00:00:55.027 --> 00:00:58.220
Key exchange and digital signatures solve different problems.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.610
Key exchange or key establishment helps parties derive shared secret material.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:08.597
Digital signatures help verify the identity of a signer and detect modification of signed data.

00:01:08.597 --> 00:01:10.991
Certificates connect public keys to identities.

00:01:10.991 --> 00:01:14.982
In a TLS or VPN setting, these roles work together.

00:01:14.982 --> 00:01:17.776
If the public-key portion becomes breakable later,

00:01:17.776 --> 00:01:23.363
captured encrypted sessions may become more vulnerable depending on protocol details and implementation choices.

00:01:23.363 --> 00:01:26.955
The planning message is not that encryption is useless.

00:01:26.955 --> 00:01:29.350
Strong, well-implemented encryption is still essential.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:34.937
The message is that long-term confidentiality depends on the assumptions behind the cryptographic system,

00:01:34.937 --> 00:01:38.130
not just on the presence of encryption labels.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.323
Teams should identify which algorithms, protocols, libraries, keys,

00:01:41.323 --> 00:01:43.318
and certificates protect long-lived data,

00:01:43.318 --> 00:01:46.260
then plan migration using standards-based and well-tested mechanisms.
